== Data and Defect Characteristics

Several hypotheses raised during the exploratory analysis remain unresolved. @section:image_obs identified substantial differences between categories in image resolution, brightness, sharpness, contrast, and texture complexity, and proposed that these characteristics may influence anomaly-detection difficulty. Future work could examine these relationships directly by comparing image characteristics with anomaly-detection performance and representation behaviour across categories. This would help determine whether characteristics such as texture complexity, local contrast, or loss of fine detail during resizing systematically affect anomaly detection rather than only appearing relevant in individual categories.

Further analysis could also be performed at the individual defect-type level. The current work primarily evaluates performance and embedding characteristics at the category level, despite substantial differences between defect types in their appearance and spatial extent. For example, @section:cls_embed_stats notes that leather and capsule have similar average defect coverage but considerably different CLS separability, suggesting that characteristics of the defects themselves may be important. Future work could therefore examine whether particular defect types consistently produce stronger or weaker representations and whether this can be related to properties such as contrast, shape, texture, or spatial extent.